\title{Orbital Rigidity: Equality at Arity Two Forces Triviality}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a group acting on a set $X$. The diagonal action of $G$ on $X \times X$ partitions the
square into \emph{orbitals}, and every orbital is contained in a product $\orb(x)\times\orb(y)$ of two
orbits; thus the orbital partition always refines the square of the orbit partition. We determine
exactly when the refinement is an equality: never, unless the action is trivial. We prove this in
three forms. First, a set-theoretic form, valid for an arbitrary group acting on an arbitrary set with
no finiteness hypotheses: every orbital is a product of orbits if and only if every group element
fixes every point. Second, a counting form for finite $G$ and $X$: writing $n=|X|$, $r$ for the number
of orbits, $s$ for the number of orbitals and $F(g)$ for the number of points fixed by $g$, Burnside's
lemma identifies $r$ and $s$ with the first and second moments of $F$ under the uniform measure on
$G$, whence the variance identity $|G|(s-r^2)=\sum_{g\in G}(F(g)-r)^2$: the rigidity defect is precisely
the variance of the fixed-point statistic. Bounding the variance below by the contribution of the
kernel $K$ and correcting the complement by Cauchy--Schwarz yields the sharp bound
$|K|(n-r)^2 \le (|G|-|K|)(s-r^2)$, and we classify the extremal actions: for a nontrivial action,
equality holds if and only if every element outside the kernel fixes the same number of points. Third,
a higher-arity form: a Chebyshev/monovariance argument gives $s_{k+1}\ge s_k r$ for the orbit counts
$s_k$ on $X^k$, hence $s_k > r^k$ for every $k\ge 2$ unless the action is trivial. Finally we isolate
the structural mechanism: orbits on $X\times Y$ are products of orbits exactly when every point
stabiliser $G_x$ remains transitive on every $G$-orbit of $Y$, and a $G$-set is never independent of
itself unless the action is trivial. We also show that no bound of the form $s-r^2\ge cn$ with an
absolute constant $c>0$ can hold.
\end{abstract}

